// embedded_probe.nv — the package's device claim, as a program that
// either builds for a Cortex-M or does not.
//
// `tests/orbit/test_glob_nv.sh` copies this and `src/glob.nv` into a
// package of its own and runs
//
//   novo build --target=nrf52-qemu src/probe.nv
//
// `walk` is NOT copied, and that is the point of the split rather than
// a convenience here: it knows about lists and the filesystem, and one
// host-only function anywhere in a compilation unit is an undefined
// symbol at embedded link time whether or not the firmware calls it.
//
// It builds, and the script BOOTS it: nothing in `glob` stands between
// it and a device.  No call allocates, every loop is a counted `for`
// over a bound computed before it starts, and the whole state of a
// match is four `Int`s in a frame.
//
// It did not build until 0.8.6, because no embedded-admitted call read
// one byte of a `Str` — `byte_of` is written with `str.byte_at_or`,
// which is that call.
// The route is documented at https://novo-lang.org/docs/stdlib/str.html

use glob

@tier(rt)
fn main() [hw, time]
    bsp.board.init()
    // The shape a device actually wants: a topic filter against a
    // topic, with no heap anywhere in the question.
    if glob.matches("sensors/**/temp", "sensors/floor2/room7/temp")
        hal.uart.write("PASS: glob-embedded\n")
    else
        hal.uart.write("FAIL: glob-embedded\n")
    loop
        core.rt.sleep_ms(1000)
